7002657,2023-05-19,"Credit reporting, credit repair services, or other personal consumer reports",Credit reporting,Improper use of your report,Reporting company used your report improperly,"The Fair Credit Reporting Act ( FCRA ) is a federal law that protects consumers ' rights to access and correct their credit reports. The FCRA also sets rules for how credit reporting companies can collect and report information about consumers. 

In XX/XX/XXXX, the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau ( CFPB ) announced new rules that would require credit reporting companies to remove certain types of collections accounts from consumers ' credit reports after two years. The new rules went into effect on XX/XX/XXXX. 

Under the new rules, credit reporting companies must remove collections accounts that are : More than two years old Not disputed by the consumer Not listed as paid in full The new rules do not apply to collections accounts that are : Still within the statute of limitations for collection Involving bankruptcy For government debts, such as student loans or taxes If you have a collections account on your credit report that is more than two years old, you can request that it be removed. You can do this by contacting the credit reporting company directly or by filing a dispute with the CFPB. 

The new FCRA laws about collections older than 2 years are a major victory for consumers. These laws will help consumers improve their credit scores and access credit. They will also make it more difficult for credit reporting companies to keep inaccurate or outdated information on consumers ' credit reports. 

Here are some additional things to keep in mind about the new FCRA laws about collections older than 2 years : The new rules only apply to collections accounts that are reported on credit reports. If a collections account is not reported on your credit report, the new rules do not apply. 
The new rules do not apply to all collections accounts. The new rules only apply to collections accounts that are more than two years old, not disputed by the consumer, and not listed as paid in full.

